    author:"OutreachNest", date:"July 2026", read:"5 min read",
    excerpt:"Not every site that says 'write for us' is worth your link. Here's the checklist we run before recommending any site.",
    content:`<p>Guest posting still works, but only when the host site is genuine. A link from a private blog network or a low-quality spam farm can quietly drag your rankings down instead of lifting them. Over time we've built a simple checklist that filters the real sites from the noise, and we run every candidate through it before it ever reaches a client.</p>
    <h3>Look past the Domain Authority number</h3>
    <p>Domain Authority is easy to inflate and easy to misread. A site can show an impressive score while receiving almost no real visitors. What matters far more is steady organic traffic from search engines, because that reflects an audience that actually exists. When a site boasts a high authority score but its traffic graph is flat at zero, treat that as a warning rather than an opportunity.</p>
    <h3>Read what they already publish</h3>
    <p>Open several recent articles and read them the way a normal visitor would. Genuine blogs have a clear subject, a consistent voice, and posts that connect to one another. Sites built purely to sell links tend to feel scattered, with unrelated topics thrown together and links inserted where they make no sense.</p>
    <h3>Study the outbound links</h3>
    <p>The links a site points to say a lot about its health. A few relevant, natural links are perfectly normal. What should make you pause is a page crowded with links to unrelated industries, or worse, links sitting next to gambling, loan, or pharmaceutical anchors that have nothing to do with the article.</p>
    <h3>Set a spam-score ceiling</h3>
    <p>We keep a firm limit on spam score and never cross it, no matter how tempting the metrics look elsewhere. The small short-term gain from a risky placement is never worth the long-term cost to your own site's reputation. Every site we hand over has already cleared this bar, so you can focus on your message instead of second-guessing the source.</p>`
