    slug:"cold-email-that-gets-replies",
    cat:"Outreach", cover:"g4", icon:"✉️",
    title:"Cold Email That Gets Replies: A Simple Framework",
    author:"OutreachNest", date:"July 2026", read:"5 min read",
    excerpt:"Long, salesy emails get ignored. Short, specific, human ones get replies. Here's the structure that lands.",
    content:`<p>Successful cold outreach has very little to do with how many emails you send and everything to do with how relevant each one feels. Ten thoughtful, personal messages will almost always beat a hundred generic blasts. The framework below is the one we keep coming back to because it works.</p>
    <h3>Keep it genuinely short</h3>
    <p>Aim for three or four sentences. If the reader has to scroll, the message is already too long. Busy people skim their inbox, so give them something they can absorb in a few seconds and decide on quickly.</p>
    <h3>Open with them, not with you</h3>
    <p>Start by referencing their work or their site rather than launching into your pitch. A line like "I came across your solar blog while researching the space" earns far more goodwill than opening with a description of how great your own service is.</p>
    <h3>Make a single, clear ask</h3>
    <p>Resist the urge to request several things at once. Ask one simple question that can be answered with a yes, and make the next step obvious and effortless. Every extra decision you hand the reader is another reason for them to close the email.</p>
    <h3>Sound like a person</h3>
    <p>Drop the corporate phrasing and write the way you'd actually speak. A real name, a warm tone, and a proper signature consistently earn more replies than a polished template that instantly reads as a mass mailing.</p>`
