    slug:"warm-up-new-email-domain",
    cat:"Deliverability", cover:"g5", icon:"🔥",
    title:"Warming Up a New Email Domain the Right Way",
    author:"OutreachNest", date:"July 2026", read:"5 min read",
    excerpt:"Send from a brand-new domain too fast and you land in spam. Here's how to build a healthy sending reputation first.",
    content:`<p>A brand-new email domain has no reputation, and inbox providers treat unknown senders with suspicion. If you start blasting outreach on day one, a large share of it will quietly land in spam folders where nobody ever sees it. A short warm-up period fixes this and is well worth the patience.</p>
    <h3>Start small and human</h3>
    <p>For the first couple of weeks, send only a handful of ordinary emails each day to people you know, and encourage them to reply. Real back-and-forth conversations are the strongest signal that your domain belongs to a genuine sender rather than a spam operation.</p>
    <h3>Get the basics in place first</h3>
    <p>Before any outreach begins, make sure your domain's authentication records are properly configured so receiving servers can confirm your mail is legitimate. Skipping this step undermines everything else you do afterward.</p>
    <h3>Grow volume gradually</h3>
    <p>Once the domain has some history, increase your daily sending slowly rather than all at once. A sudden jump from a few emails to hundreds looks exactly like the behavior spam filters are built to catch.</p>
    <h3>Watch the signals that matter</h3>
    <ul><li>Keep your messages relevant so people don't mark them as spam.</li><li>Make it easy for uninterested recipients to opt out.</li><li>Pay attention to replies and engagement, which steadily build trust.</li></ul>
    <p>Treat the first few weeks as an investment. A properly warmed domain quietly pays you back with every campaign that actually reaches the inbox.</p>`
